702 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Flowchart detailing the selection process for a systematic review. Records identified: MEDLINE \(1680\), CENTRAL \(527\), Web of Science \(272\). After removing duplicates, 2455 records were screened. 2406 records were excluded as irrelevant. 49 full-text articles were assessed for eligibility; 30 excluded for reasons like not reporting outcomes of interest \(9\), duplicates \(2\), alternative treatments \(6\), unavailable full texts \(5\), and improper methods \(8\). Finally, 19 articles were included in both qualitative and quantitative syntheses. The flowchart sections are labeled as Identification, Screening, Eligibility, and Included.) /K 771 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 224 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
703 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Bar chart depicting risk of bias across several categories. Categories include random sequence generation, allocation concealment, blinding of participants, outcome assessment, incomplete outcome data, selective reporting, and other bias. Each bar shows proportions of low \(green\), unclear \(yellow\), and high \(red\) risk of bias. Key indicates colors: green for low, yellow for unclear, and red for high risk.) /K 772 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 230 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
704 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Risk of bias assessment chart for multiple studies from 2001 to 2024. Categories include random sequence generation, allocation concealment, blinding, incomplete outcome data, selective reporting, and other biases. Symbols used are plus \(low risk\), minus \(high risk\), and question mark \(unclear risk\).) /K 773 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 231 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
705 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing the mean differences in baseline and end measurements for two subgroups: Case \(Peptide\) and Control \(Placebo\). Each study is represented, with squares indicating the mean difference and horizontal lines denoting the 95% confidence interval. The pooled estimates are shown as diamonds with associated prediction intervals. Random effects models are applied, with heterogeneity statistics provided. The graph emphasizes the comparative effects of peptide versus placebo treatments.) /K 774 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 232 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
706 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing the mean difference and confidence intervals of peptide studies. Subgroups include Polypeptides, Tripeptides, and Oligopeptides. It includes baseline means, end means, and weights. The plot displays the random effects model with heterogeneity statistics, prediction intervals, and confidence intervals. The overall effect test is not significant at p = 0.12.) /K 775 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 233 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
707 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot displaying the mean differences in studies comparing peptide and placebo groups. Each study is listed with baseline and end mean values. Red squares represent mean differences with error bars indicating the ninety-five percent confidence intervals. Studies are grouped by treatment and control, with summary estimates shown as diamonds. Random effects model results and heterogeneity statistics are included at the plot bottom, indicating significant findings.) /K 776 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 234 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
708 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing mean differences between baseline and end measurements across studies grouped by polypeptides, oligopeptides, and tripeptides. Each study is represented with a red square and confidence interval line, indicating variability, with summary estimates shown as black diamonds. The plot displays heterogeneity statistics and weights for each subgroup, with polypeptides showing the highest weight. The overall effect is significant with a test result of z = 6.14 and a prediction interval indicated.) /K 777 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 235 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
709 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot comparing peptide and placebo groups across several studies. The plot includes baseline mean, end mean, mean differences, and 95% confidence intervals for each study. The random effects model shows overall mean differences with confidence intervals, highlighting significant heterogeneity. The plot indicates a prediction interval and tests for the overall effect and subgroup differences.) /K 778 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 236 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
710 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (A forest plot showing a meta-analysis of three subgroups: oligopeptides, polypeptides, and tripeptides. Each study lists baseline and end means, mean differences, confidence intervals, and weights. The oligopeptides subgroup has four studies, polypeptides have eight, and tripeptides have one. The plot includes a diamond representing the random effects model for each subgroup and overall. Heterogeneity and statistical test results are noted, with total weights and confidence intervals in bold.) /K 779 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 236 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
711 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing mean differences in baseline and end measurements between intervention and control groups across multiple studies. Each study's mean difference with 95% confidence intervals is represented by squares for individual studies and diamonds for overall effects. Subgroups are Cases \(Intervention\) and Control \(Placebo\). The weighted average for each subgroup and overall are provided. Heterogeneity is indicated with I-squared values; ninety-nine percent for cases and eighty-seven percent for controls. The plot includes a central vertical line at zero, indicating no difference.) /K 780 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 237 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
712 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot displaying mean differences for three subgroups: tripeptides, oligopeptides, and polypeptides. Squares represent individual study estimates with corresponding confidence intervals as horizontal lines. Diamonds indicate pooled estimates per subgroup. The overall random effects model shows a mean difference with prediction interval. Heterogeneity and weight percentages are provided for each study and subgroup.) /K 781 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 237 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
713 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing mean differences in a study with intervention and placebo groups. Each study \(Murad, Lin, Lee\) is represented with red squares and confidence intervals. Subgroup analyses for intervention and placebo show varying effect sizes. The overall effect size and heterogeneity statistics are displayed at the bottom.) /K 782 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 238 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
714 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing mean differences in polypeptide studies by Lin \(2021\) and Lee \(2023\). Mean baseline to end values are 63.80 to 67.20 and 65.47 to 71.28, respectively. Combined effect size is 5.0 with a 95% confidence interval of 2.18 to 6.90. Heterogeneity is high at 85%, and the test for overall effect is significant with z = 3.78, p < 0.01.) /K 783 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 238 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
715 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing a meta-analysis of interventions and control groups across four studies: Murad \(2001\), Lee \(2023\), Lin \(2021\), and Kalil \(2018\). The plot includes baseline and end-mean values, mean differences with 95% confidence intervals, and weight percentages for each study. Results are divided into intervention and placebo subgroups. Random effects models assess heterogeneity, with I-squared values and P-values indicated. A prediction interval is also displayed. The overall effect size is visualized by a red diamond.) /K 784 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 239 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
716 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing mean differences between baseline and end mean values for three studies by Lee \(2023\), Lin \(2021\), and Kalil \(2018\) within a polypeptides subgroup. The plot displays confidence intervals and weights, with a random effects model predicting an overall mean difference of 2.88 to 3.66. Heterogeneity is zero percent, indicating limited variability across studies.) /K 785 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 239 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
717 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot displaying the mean differences between intervention and placebo groups in studies by Taub \(2018\) and Bolke \(2019\). Individual study results are shown with red squares, size indicating study weight, alongside confidence intervals. Random effects models and prediction intervals are illustrated with black diamonds. Heterogeneity statistics are included, with I-squared values and p-values for subgroup differences and overall effect tests.) /K 786 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 239 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
718 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot depicting a meta-analysis of oligopeptides. Two studies are compared: Taub \(2018\) with a baseline-mean difference of 0.99 and an end-mean of 1.01, and Bolke \(2019\) with 35.70 and 44.00, respectively. The mean differences and confidence intervals are shown. Overall, heterogeneity is high with \\\( I^2 = 96\\% \\\). The plot shows a combined mean difference and 95% confidence intervals for each study with weights of 52.2% and 47.8%, respectively.) /K 787 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 240 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
719 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot displaying subgroup analysis of oral \(Bolke, 2019\) and topical \(Taub, 2018\) interventions. Oral subgroup has a mean difference of 8.3 with a 95% CI of 4.88 to 11.72, accounting for 47.8% weight. The topical subgroup shows a mean difference of 0.02 with a 95% CI of -0.03 to 0.06, contributing 52.2% weight. Overall mean difference is -4.14 to 12.08 with 100% weight. Random effects model shows high heterogeneity \(I² = 96%\).) /K 788 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 240 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
endobj
720 0 obj
<< /A << /O /Layout /Placement /Block >> /Alt (Forest plot showing the mean differences of several studies, each represented by a red square, with confidence intervals. All studies, except one showing ninety-five percent I2 inconsistency, have one hundred percent. A summary diamond at the bottom indicates the random effects model, centered near zero.) /K 789 0 R /P 245 0 R /Pg 241 0 R /S /Figure /Type /StructElem >>
